  "Implicit_Disease_Burden" [label="(0,0,0) Source: (0, 0, 0) Transcription: Induction-reasoning: Case RRP is epidemic, and Common RRP is debilitating, so RRP imposes substantial disease burden."];
  "Implicit_Disease_Link" [label="(0,0,0) Source: (0, 0, 0) Transcription: Induction-reasoning: Case HPV is epidemic, and Common HPV-6/11 cause RRP, so RRP is part of a global epidemic."];
  "Implicit_Treatment_Limit" [label="(0,0,0) Source: (0, 0, 0) Transcription: Induction-reasoning: Case Surgery causes morbidity, and Common Injury risk increases, so current surgical treatment has significant limitations."];
  "NROOT" [label="(0,0,0) This study investigates INO-3107, a DNA immunotherapy encoding HPV-6/11 antigens plus an IL-12 adjuvant, for patients with HPV-6/11-related recurrent respiratory papillomatosis, evaluating whether it can safely elicit HPV-specific immune responses and provide clinical efficacy.", fillcolor="lightyellow", style="filled"];
  "NROOT_COMMON" [label="(0,0,0) When a burdensome HPV-6/11-related disease has limited surgical management and immunologic rationale for intervention, a completed trial of a rational DNA immunotherapy candidate motivates reporting safety, immune-response, and clinical-efficacy outcomes."];
  "R10" [label="(0,0,0) INO-3107, a DNA immunotherapy encoding HPV-6/11 and IL-12, is a rational candidate to study in this immunotherapy context."];
  "R11" [label="(0,0,0) Because HPV-specific immunotherapy is a plausible treatment-improvement approach and INO-3107 is a rational DNA immunotherapy candidate, a study of INO-3107 for RRP is scientifically motivated."];
  "R2" [label="(0,0,0) RRP lesions impair voice production and breathing, and papillomas can rarely undergo malignant transformation, so RRP has clinically important morbidity and some oncologic risk rather than uniformly high clinical risk."];
  "R4" [label="(0,0,0) Because RRP imposes substantial disease burden and has clinically important morbidity and some oncologic risk, it represents a serious clinical problem that contributes to the rationale for better management."];
  "R5" [label="(0,0,0) A serious RRP clinical burden together with morbidity from repeated surgical management supports the need to investigate improved therapeutic approaches."];
  "R7" [label="(0,0,0) Prior DNA immunotherapies showing potent immune responses, together with other immune-based evidence affecting disease state, support investigating DNA immunotherapy approaches as plausible candidates for HPV-associated disease rather than proving broad clinical efficacy."];
  "R9" [label="(0,0,0) Given the need to investigate improved therapy for RRP and evidence of suppressive immune-related functions within papillomas, HPV-specific immunotherapy is a plausible approach to improve current treatment."];
  "S1" [label="(1,0,0) Source: (1, 0, 0) Transcription: Case: HPV-associated diseases are an emerging global epidemic."];
  "S10" [label="(10,0,0) Source: (10, 0, 0) Transcription: Case: Previous DNA immunotherapies demonstrated potent immune responses."];
  "S11" [label="(11,0,0) Source: (11, 0, 0) Transcription: Common: Other groups support an immune-based impact on disease state."];
  "S12" [label="(12,0,0) Source: (12, 0, 0) Transcription: Currently there is INO-3107, a DNA immunotherapy encoding HPV-6/11 and IL-12."];
  "S2" [label="(2,0,0) Source: (2, 0, 0) Transcription: Common: The role of HPV-6 and HPV-11 in RRP etiology is well-established."];
